<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
    <string>LocalBook needs microphone access for voice-to-text transcription using Whisper</string>

    <!-- Linked Folders (2026-09-16). TCC only PROMPTS for these locations when
         the bundle declares a usage description; without one the read is
         refused and the user never sees a dialog to approve. The folder is
         always chosen by the user in a picker first — these strings just let
         macOS ask on their behalf, and explain why.
         The Python backend reads the folder as a child process, and TCC
         attributes that to the responsible parent — this bundle. -->
    <key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>LocalBook reads folders you choose to link, so new files in them become sources in your notebooks. It never modifies your files.</string>
    <key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>LocalBook reads folders you choose to link, so new files in them become sources in your notebooks. It never modifies your files.</string>
    <key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
    <string>LocalBook reads folders you choose to link, so new files in them become sources in your notebooks. It never modifies your files.</string>
    <key>NSRemovableVolumesUsageDescription</key>
    <string>LocalBook reads folders you choose to link, including ones on external drives. It never modifies your files.</string>

    <!-- Multi-machine sync, K-1 / LB-12 (2026-09-29). macOS refuses local-network
         discovery outright when the bundle declares no usage description, and the
         refusal is silent — the peer simply never appears. entitlements.plist
         already grants com.apple.security.network.server; these are the Info.plist
         half, which was the only piece actually missing.
         Nothing here gates data access: per D20 the volume password is an ordinary
         disk-image password held by macOS, so an ad-hoc build still opens its own
         data. These keys only affect finding other Macs. -->
    <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
    <string>LocalBook finds your other Macs on this network to keep your notebooks in sync. Your data is sent directly between your own machines and never to a server.</string>
    <key>NSBonjourServices</key>
    <array>
        <string>_localbook-sync._tcp</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
